  <p>
    The stress axis is a relay with three stations: hypothalamus, pituitary, adrenal. The
    middle station speaks melanocortin. Pituitary cells called corticotrophs make the
    precursor <a href="/pomc">POMC</a>, cut <a href="/acth">ACTH</a> out of it, and release it
    into the blood, where it tells the adrenal cortex to make cortisol.
    <strong>Cushing's disease</strong> is what happens when a small tumour of those cells
    keeps sending that message, mostly regardless of whether anyone needs it.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>Syndrome versus disease</h2>
  <p>
    The naming trips everyone up, so it is worth getting straight. <em>Cushing's syndrome</em>
    is the clinical state of long-term cortisol excess, whatever is causing it, including
    glucocorticoid medication. <em>Cushing's disease</em> is one cause of it: a pituitary
    tumour secreting ACTH. Among the endogenous causes it is the most common, accounting for
    the majority of cases.<Cite n={2} /> The rest divide between tumours elsewhere in the body
    that make ACTH — ectopic ACTH syndrome — and adrenal tumours that make cortisol with no
    ACTH involved at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    That gives the split clinicians actually work with. If ACTH is driving the cortisol, the
    condition is <em>ACTH-dependent</em>, and the question becomes where the ACTH is coming
    from. If ACTH is suppressed, the adrenal is acting alone. Only the first group is a
    melanocortin story.
  </p>

  <h2>A healthy gland following a bad signal</h2>
  <p>
    ACTH is a melanocortin, a sibling of the peptides that tan skin and set appetite. It is
    the only one that can activate <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>, the receptor on the
    adrenal cortex, because that receptor demands a stretch of the ACTH chain the shorter MSH
    peptides do not have. When ACTH binds, the adrenal makes cortisol, and under sustained
    stimulation it also grows.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Normally cortisol feeds back to the pituitary and the signal eases. The tumour in
    Cushing's disease is characteristically less sensitive to that feedback than normal
    corticotrophs, so ACTH keeps coming at cortisol levels that should have shut it
    off.<Cite n={2} /> The result is the reverse of{' '}
    <a href="/addisons-disease">Addison's disease</a> and the cousin of{' '}
    <a href="/congenital-adrenal-hyperplasia">congenital adrenal hyperplasia</a>: in all
    three, ACTH is high, but here the adrenal can do everything it is asked, and does.
  </p>

  <h2>The pigment clue</h2>
  <p>
    The first thirteen residues of ACTH are <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>. So wherever ACTH
    runs high enough, the pigment receptor <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> can pick it up,
    and skin darkens. In ordinary Cushing's disease ACTH is often only modestly raised and
    darkening is not a leading feature. It is more characteristic of ectopic ACTH syndrome,
    where a non-pituitary tumour can produce ACTH in much larger amounts.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    The most striking example is <strong>Nelson's syndrome</strong>. If both adrenals are
    removed to control cortisol, the pituitary tumour loses what feedback it still had and can
    progress, with ACTH climbing and the skin darkening as it does.<Cite n={4} /> It is the
    same physiology as Addison's bronzing, arrived at by surgery: no cortisol brake, a great
    deal of ACTH, and a skin receptor that cannot tell ACTH from the tanning signal.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is treated, and where the receptor fits</h2>
  <p>
    The recommended first-line treatment for Cushing's disease is surgery to remove the
    pituitary tumour.<Cite n={1} /> When surgery does not cure the condition or cannot be
    done, the options include further surgery, radiotherapy and medical therapy, and the
    existing medicines act at three different points: on the pituitary tumour, on the
    adrenal's steroid-making enzymes, or on the glucocorticoid receptor in the body's
    tissues.<Cite n={1} /> None of them blocks the receptor that sits between the signal and
    the response.
  </p>
  <p>
    That gap is what an MC2R antagonist is meant to fill. Block ACTH at the adrenal and, in
    principle, it no longer matters whether the ACTH comes from the pituitary or from an
    ectopic tumour. The idea has reached people only in small, short, open-label cohorts run
    with the US National Institutes of Health, in which an investigational oral antagonist
    lowered cortisol within days and participants then needed cortisol
    replacement.<Cite n={5} /> That is an early pharmacology result from a handful of
    patients, reported by the sponsor. It is not evidence of long-term benefit, and it leaves
    open the questions a longer trial has to answer, including what a pituitary tumour does
    when its target organ stops responding. The wider class is covered on the{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-antagonists">antagonists hub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Cushing's disease is not treated through the melanocortin system today. It belongs on this
    site because it is the cleanest demonstration that ACTH is a working melanocortin: one
    peptide from POMC, one receptor at the adrenal, and a whole-body illness when the peptide
    is overproduced. The pigment sign, where it appears, is the family resemblance showing
    through. For the hormone, see <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>; for the receptor,{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>; and for how this compares with every other condition
    the system touches, the <a href="/conditions">conditions map</a>.
  </p>

